{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on 90f926ad: run the isolated LIST1 owner once, then one\nwhole gate with passive memory samples. No setting changes.\n\n1. Run `apps/room/page/test/list1-source.scope.test.ts` once at\n   unchanged ff0eee7d, in its maintained Native project and pool,\n   with the original 120000 ms limit. I accept your plan as written.\n2. From the retained v5b output, name the process that aborted: the\n   Node pool worker or a workerd process. Name the owner that it\n   ran at the abort. Report both with the isolated result.\n3. If the isolated owner fails, the defect is real. Repair it under\n   BR-13, rerun that owner, then run the gate.\n4. If the isolated owner passes, run one whole gate at the same\n   source. During it, sample the resident memory of the run's own\n   node and workerd processes every 10 seconds to a file outside\n   the checkout. Use `ps` by PID from the owned process groups. Set\n   no heap flag, no `NODE_OPTIONS`, no worker limit and no deadline.\n5. If that gate passes complete, it is BR-13's gate for review. The\n   note keeps v5b as a failed gate, names the heap abort and gives\n   the peak sample of each process kind. The peak is the baseline\n   for later heads.\n6. If the heap abort recurs, stop. Run no further gate. Report the\n   peak samples, the process and the owner at the abort. I then\n   rule on a comparison run at main 19ddc868 with the same samples.\n\nThe samples exist to answer one question: whether this head raised\nthe Native stage's memory, or the stage already runs near its\nlimit. Do not add a landed memory check or a new test for it.\n\nHeads B and D use the slot while the gate is not ready. BR-13's one\ngate takes the slot when its isolated run has passed.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}